Release Notes:v1.1.4 09.05.93 added -newer and !-newer                use -newer to find files newer than specified file                use !-newer to find files older than specified file                Examples:                Print files in current dir (and sub-directories) that                are newer than makefile.                  find . -newer makefile -print                Print filename for all files on /h0 that are older than                my_file.                  find /h0 !-newer my_file -printv1.1.5 09.18.93 added -exec and -ok                use -exec instead of -print to execute a command on match                -ok will ask if it is ok to run command                Examples:                Sort all directories on /h0.                  find /h0 -type d -exec sortdir {} \;                {} is replaced by the search string.                \; is not required and is included for compatibility with                   the Unix command.  Also \ is the shell escape character                   which only has meaning with Unix style shells (ie. bash)                   and is used to escape the ; from the shell, because the                   Unix find requires the ;.